  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 145 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nLatest models have shown the exit of the high pressure slowing\ndown and thus the approaching low and corresponding precip\nexpected has also slowed down. However, Friday, later during \nthe day we should start to see precip gradually push in from the\nwest and overspread the region by the late overnight hours with\nprecip potential lingering through much of the day Saturday.\nDeep moisture with strong WAA from a moderate LLJ as well as\nupper level shortwave energy the forcing and moisture are plenty\nenough. This is all in addition to very strong Fgen. While \nstrong storms are not expected with this system, the PWATs over \n2 inches for much of the event lend itself to the thought that \nit could be a very efficient rainfall with some lightning \nintermittently. \n\nThe prolonged nature of this event in combination with its \npotentially high efficiency does yield some concerns about \nflooding potential, especially in the areas with highest Fgen. \nWhile flash flooding given the style of the event is unlikely we\ncannot completely rule out flash flooding style impacts with \npotential for significant ponding of water. However, generally \nthis is not expected to cause major concerns. Rivers are not \nexpected to be significantly impacted either given the lower \nriver levels and dryness of soils overall which should allow \n(with non- thunderstorm rain) for decent infiltration and less \nrunoff. There still remains some questions about whether models \nare overdoing the potential rain amounts but the result of this \nshould largely come down to track, speed and the efficiency of \nthe system which is still somewhat in doubt so we will have to \ncontinue to monitor these aspects to better understand what \nthis event might look like. In addition, latest models are also \nprolonging the exit of rain as well keeping rain potential into \nand potentially even through Sunday as a secondary low may lift \nfar enough north to bring additional high efficiency precip to \nparts of the CWA though its worth mentioning that there is \nhigher uncertainty due to the interactions with the developing \nhigh in southern Canada with the low. \n\nThis may very well continue into Monday as well as what appears\nto be a tertiary low ejecting from the central US brings another\nround of potential precip to southern WI. Uncertainty remains\nhigh out this far but models are in fairly good agreement on\nthis. This could make for 4 straight days of rain and potential\nfor higher end amounts. This will be something to watch as it \ncould introduce potential for 3-5 inches of rain with pockets of\nover 7 inches from Friday through Monday. Rain Monday does\nappear to at least be lighter in nature due to the PWATs coming\nback down but PWATs likely remain high (>1.75 in) through \nSunday.\n\nInto midweek, forecast becomes much more uncertain and while\nmuch drier conditions should be expected generally it is not do\nsay that we will not see any storms or showers just that we do\nnot expect an all day rain situation. Some upper level\ntrough/shortwaves could play a role in exactly what to expect\nfor the midweek period with chances for showers/storms possible.\n\nKuroski",
